ingapore Life But in October, an unidentified person contacted Danish newspaper Politiken in English via an encrypted messaging service and claimed responsibility.The supposed hacker said they had found an entry point into the register within a few hours by using the login credentials of a former employee of a small Danish company. They said the employee’s password was 123456.In September, the Civil Security Agency raised the threat level for cyberattacks against Denmark to “high”.There has been a surge in cyberattacks, notably targeting supermarket refrigeration systems.There has also been a series of false bomb threats against schools and planes across Europe, which could potentially be part of a hybrid warfare campaign.That theory has not been confirmed, however, due to a lack of evidence. AFP

